+# Forking Base Locally
+
+This checkout is Foundry-flavored Scaffold-ETH 2. For work that reads Base
+USDC at `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`, start the local node as
+a fork of Base, not just as any responsive local chain.
+
+## What the old note gets wrong
+
+The old note said:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork base
+cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+That check only proves that a JSON-RPC node is answering. In this repo it does
+not prove the upstream fork is Base, because the fork target and the local chain
+id are separate things.
+
+The relevant repo lines are:
+
+```json
+// package.json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork",
+```
+
+```json
+// packages/foundry/package.json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+```make
+# packages/foundry/Makefile
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+```toml
+# packages/foundry/foundry.toml
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+localhost = "http://127.0.0.1:8545"
+```
+
+The Foundry workspace script reads `${1:-mainnet}`. With Yarn 4, a single extra
+argument like `base` is exposed as `$0`, not `$1`, so `yarn fork base` leaves
+`$1` empty and falls back to `mainnet`. That starts an Anvil fork using the
+`mainnet` RPC alias, i.e. Ethereum mainnet, while the Makefile still forces the
+local chain id to `31337`.
+
+So `cast chain-id` can return `31337` for:
+
+- a Base fork
+- an Ethereum mainnet fork
+- a plain local Anvil chain
+
+It is not a Base-fork health check.
+
+The USDC reads come back empty because `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`
+is the Base USDC contract address. On the accidental Ethereum mainnet fork there
+is no Base USDC contract at that address, so calls against it have no contract
+state/code to read.
+
+## Start the Base fork
+
+Use the Makefile target directly and pass the fork URL alias as a make variable:
+
+```bash
+make -C packages/foundry fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+This runs the repo's fork target as:
+
+```bash
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The `base` alias is defined in `packages/foundry/foundry.toml` as
+`https://mainnet.base.org`. When run from `packages/foundry`, Anvil resolves it
+and prints a fork banner with:
+
+```text
+Endpoint:       https://mainnet.base.org
+Chain ID:       31337
+```
+
+The `31337` is the local Anvil chain id requested by the Makefile; it is not the
+upstream Base chain id.
+
+## Sanity check the fork
+
+Check the chain-specific contract we actually depend on:
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  "symbol()(string)" \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  "decimals()(uint8)" \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+Expected output on a Base fork:
+
+```text
+"USDC"
+6
+```
+
+For an even lower-level check, the code at that address must be non-empty:
+
+```bash
+cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+Expected: a long `0x...` bytecode value, not `0x`.

